ludes that verb as possible  now in link we have the opportunity to  say first of all if you save a word you  will save a chunk automatically the  system saves a chunk and when you look  at that word that you've saved say this  verb you'll see five or six or seven  examples of that verb being used and  you'll see which words it is used with  so the idea of phrasal verbs is that  certain I mean it's true not only for  verbs but certain verbs are used  together with certain other words  typically with prepositions sometimes  that changes the meaning or gives it  different meaning for example I know a  lot of non-native speakers of English  will often say I found out a book at the  library instead of I found a book at the  library okay and the word find out means  more to discover whereas to find is to  come across something come across  there's another phrasal verb come very  common verb you come by something you  come across something and you come at  something so that the preposition behind  the verb will sort of give a different  nuance and sometimes a completely  different meaning to the verb so you  have to learn that combination as a  different term almost but you have to  begin by being conscious of which words  are used together with other words and  in particular with with words so one of  the things that I have done for learning  other languages like Slavic languages is  is to tag the words that I save so if  you are learning English at link you  tagged every verb that you feel is  involved in phrasal verbs before we get  into that let me say that because a lot  of our members at link are saving  phrases it is very likely that if you  come across it's almost certain that if  you come across a verb that's used in a  longer phrase a common longer phrase  some  we'll have saved that as a phrase so you  can see the list of phrases that have  been saved by other users and you can  choose one of them and save it to your  database so you can actually save either  verbs that are commonly used in phrasal  verbs or the phrases to your database  then you can tag them you can tag them  as phrasal verbs so just for the fun of  it  I went and tagged a bunch of verbs that  are commonly used in phrasal verbs so  you have turn okay so if I look at turn  and I open that I see that the samples  turn in turn okay here's one in turn  which isn't quite a phrasal verb turn  those three things into turn something  into turn it into practical example turn  on the TV so those are here again or  turn about and travel so just by saving  the word turn I pick up automatically  six phrases using the word turn or if I  know I also chose no as a phrasal verb  in other words a verb that gets involved  in phrasal verbs you can also save you  know the past tense new but most of us  know that being know their limits I know  my okay know well so this one is less  useful let's take for example give give  it to them give it back give it away so  give us a clue so you know I could go on  and on here get which is a big one in  phrasal verbs and get to a modicum get  it in front of get encouraged get to  sleep get back so what I'm suggesting  here is in order to get better at  phrasal verbs you have to be exposed to  them so you have to pay attention when  you're reading and listening if you hear  a phrasal verb in other words a phrase  using a certain verb you have to get  used to the way that verb and certain  other words function together as a unit  so you have to pay attention so one way  of doing  you tagged a bunch of these say it link  and then you review them as a group  alright so the other thing is getting  back to find out I I if I were a  non-native speaker I would get to what I  can understand the phrasal verbs as much  as possible but I would avoid using them  because I'm there's a chance I'll get  them wrong so rather than saying find  out I would use discover and generally  speaking phrasal verbs are less formal  than what is often their Latin origin  equivalent and so in formal writing it's  in fact often better not to use the  phrasal verb so I would treat phrasal  verbs not as some great big exceptional  thing it's simply an example of the rule  that in any language we have there is a  tendency for certain words to be used  together with other words this is  sometimes referred to as collocations  which is again a difficult term we  needn't learn we just need to know that  certain words are used with other words  and that the more naturally you put  these words together the more the way  you put words together is typical of how  words are used the more native-like  you're gonna sound therefore you should  save words or you should excuse me  phrases you should focus on phrases when  you save a word you should pay attention  to which words are used with that word  and that will help you not only on  phrasal verbs per se but even such  things as you know listen to so many  times you hear the non-native speaker  say I listen him  whereas I listen to him okay you can  tell him but you can't listen him you  have to listen to him so that it's not  just phrasal verbs it's the idea of  which verbs take which prepositions  which verbs are used with other ver  other words and that's something that  should be a major part of your language  learning and again not only for English  but for any language focusing on which  words are used with which other words  and that will make you you know a more  natural speaker of the language because  you you can't you can't just get a book  of all the key phrasal verbs and read  through the book and hope that somehow  you'll remember them you have to develop  habits through your listening and  reading so that you naturally start to  use these words correctly so there I  hope that's useful and because I'm not a  learner of English I would like to know  if in fact that is useful and maybe some  people could tell me what specifically  they find difficult about phrasal verbs  alright